ple compression formats on same data may impact I/O performance.\"\n    affected_tasks: [\"task:individuals\", \"task:individuals_merge\", \"task:mutation_overlap\", \"task:frequency\"]\n    affected_data: [\"data:individuals_output_chunk\", \"data:individuals_merged\"]\n    suggested_investigation: \"Profile tar/gzip overhead vs. compute time. Consider uncompressed tar or direct file sharing between individuals and merge if temporary storage permits.\"\n\n  - concern_id: \"io:individuals_merge_recompression\"\n    potential_concern: \"unnecessary_serialization\"\n    severity: \"low\"\n    description: \"individuals_merge performs tar extract-then-recompress-to-tar. If individuals task output were not compressed or used tar directly (without intermediate gzip), this step could reduce I/O overhead. Currently forces decompression and recompression of same data.\"\n    affected_tasks: [\"task:individuals_merge\"]\n    affected_data: [\"data:individuals_output_chunk\", \"data:individuals_merged\"]\n    suggested_investigation: \"Consider directly writing merged tar files from individuals tasks rather than individual chunks, or eliminating intermediate compression if storage is not constrained.\"\n\n  - concern_id: \"io:shared_file_multiple_readers\"\n    potential_concern: \"metadata_overhead\"\n    severity: \"low\"\n    description: \"individuals_merged and sifted_variants files are each read by two analysis tasks (mutation_overlap and frequency). Without explicit data replication or caching, both tasks may open the same file from shared storage, potentially causing metadata server load if storage is remote (e.g., Lustre, NFS).\"\n    affected_tasks: [\"task:mutation_overlap\", \"task:frequency\"]\n    affected_data: [\"data:individuals_merged\", \"data:sifted_variants\"]\n    suggested_investigation: \"Monitor metadata server load during analysis stage. Consider replicating shared files to local task storage if remote filesystem is bottleneck, or using collective I/O patterns if MPI is available.\"\n\n  - concern_id: \"io:python_posix_scale\"\n    potential_concern: \"parallel_access_risk\"\n    severity: \"low\"\n    description: \"All tasks use Python serial I/O (POSIX file operations) rather than MPI-IO or HDF5 parallel I/O. At scale (hundreds of parallel jobs), may cause issues on shared filesystem if all jobs simultaneously open/read/write files. Metadata operations (open, stat, close) on shared files can saturate metadata servers.\"\n    affected_tasks: [\"task:individuals\", \"task:individuals_merge\", \"task:sifting\", \"task:mutation_overlap\", \"task:frequency\"]\n    affected_data: [\"data:vcf_input\", \"data:sift_annotation_vcf\", \"data:individuals_output_chunk\", \"data:individuals_merged\", \"data:sifted_variants\"]\n    suggested_investigation: \"Profile filesystem metadata server load during full-scale runs. Consider implementing file striping strategies or local caching if metadata becomes bottleneck.\"\n\n  - concern_id: \"io:large_memory_arrays\"\n    potential_concern: \"memory_intensive_pattern\"\n    severity: \"medium\"\n    description: \"mutation_overlap and frequency tasks read entire individuals files and SIFT scores into numpy arrays for in-memory matrix operations. With thousands of individuals and variants, this creates large array allocations (potentially multi-GB per task). No evidence of streaming or chunked processing.\"\n    affected_tasks: [\"task:mutation_overlap\", \"task:frequency\"]\n    affected_data: [\"data:individuals_merged\", \"data:sifted_variants\"]\n    suggested_investigation: \"Verify memory requirements scale sub-quadratically with individual count. If memory scales quadratically (N×N overlap matrices), consider chunked processing or sparse matrix representations for large populations.\"\n\nexecution_profiles:\n  - profile_name: \"full_analysis\"\n    terminal_task_id: \"task:frequency\"\n    description: \"Complete execution of the 1000 Genomes workflow producing both mutation overlap and frequency analysis results for all specified chromosomes and populations.\"\n    required_task_set: [\"task:individuals\", \"task:individuals_merge\", \"task:sifting\", \"task:mutation_overlap\", \"task:frequency\"]\n    conditional_inclusions: []\n    output_dataset_ids: [\"data:mutation_overlap_results\", \"data:frequency_results\"]\n\ntranslation_metadata:\n  source_format: \"pegasus_dax_generator\"\n  source_file: \"daxgen.py\"\n  translation_tool: \"claude-haiku-4-5\"\n  translation_timestamp: \"2026-03-11T14:30:00Z\"\n  unmapped_fields: []\n  round_trip_gaps: []\n  quality_warnings: []\n  field_confidence_records:\n    - field_path: \"metadata.workflow_description\"\n      filled_by: \"claude\"\n      confidence_level: \"high\"\n      confidence_reason: \"Directly extracted from README.md and validated against task descriptions.\"\n      requires_human_review: false\n    - field_path: \"tasks[*].parallelism_model.type\"\n      filled_by: \"claude\"\n      confidence_level: \"high\"\n      confidence_reason: \"Inferred from code structure (loops over chromosomes/populations, --individuals-jobs parameter) and description in README.md.\"\n      requires_human_review: false\n    - field_path: \"tasks[*].io_access_type\"\n      filled_by: \"claude\"\n      confidence_level: \"high\"\n      confidence_reason: \"Inferred from Python executables (standard Python file I/O via open/read/write) and lack of MPI/HDF5 parallel I/O imports in daxgen.py and executable scripts.\"\n      requires_human_review: false\n    - field_path: \"tasks[*].restart_model\"\n      filled_by: \"claude\"\n      confidence_level: \"medium\"\n      confidence_reason: \"Inferred from task design patterns (chunk-based processing for individuals tasks, no explicit checkpoint/resume logic found). All tasks appear idempotent (output file overwrite behavior).\"\n      requires_human_review: false\n    - field_path: \"tasks[*].behavioral_notes\"\n      filled_by: \"claude\"\n      conf